</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{SAE}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{SAE}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{SAE}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>dec</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{SAE}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{SAE}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{SAE}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>dec</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the SAE module.
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ReLU}(\cdot):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ReLU}(\cdot):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an element-wise ReLU activation.
Each <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="12.01" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.38px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 68.8 12.01" width="68.8"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,12.01) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(34.4,0) translate(0,1.38)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9D9FF;" fill="#D9D9FF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 30.25 10.62 L -30.25 10.62 C -32.54 10.62 -34.4 8.77 -34.4 6.47 L -34.4 2.77 C -34.4 0.47 -32.54 -1.38 -30.25 -1.38 L 30.25 -1.38 C 32.54 -1.38 34.4 0.47 34.4 2.77 L 34.4 6.47 C 34.4 8.77 32.54 10.62 30.25 10.62 Z M -34.4 -1.38"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -29.79 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.31em;--ltx-fo-height:0.67em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.24" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.24)" width="59.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">activation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> dimension is treated as a potentially interpretable feature, and the <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="12.01" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.38px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 49.62 12.01" width="49.62"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,12.01) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(24.81,0) translate(0,1.38)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E2E2E2;" fill="#E2E2E2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 20.66 10.62 L -20.66 10.62 C -22.95 10.62 -24.81 8.77 -24.81 6.47 L -24.81 2.77 C -24.81 0.47 -22.95 -1.38 -20.66 -1.38 L 20.66 -1.38 C 22.95 -1.38 24.81 0.47 24.81 2.77 L 24.81 6.47 C 24.81 8.77 22.95 10.62 20.66 10.62 Z M -24.81 -1.38"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -20.2 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.67em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.24" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.24)" width="40.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">matrix</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> maps each feature dimension to its feature vector in the representation space.
This module is trained so that the activations are as sparse as possible with a sparsity loss to disentangle the potentially polysemantic input neurons.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transcoder.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, as an alternative to SAEs and perhaps the closest attempt to this study, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Transcoders</span> have recently been proposed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
This approximates the original FF by training a sparse MLP as a proxy to predict FF output <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>FF</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> from FF input <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>FF</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its internal activations (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{TC}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TC</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{TC}}}</annotation></semantics></math>) are evaluated in the same way as the standard SAE.
Still, their work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> did not clearly evaluate how interpretable the original FF’s internal activations are, and this work complements this overlooked question.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Feed-Forward Layer as Key-Value Memories</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Feed-forward layers in Transformers once project the FF input <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>FF</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>FF</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{\mathrm{model}}&lt;d_{\mathrm{FF}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>FF</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{\mathrm{model}}&lt;d_{\mathrm{FF}})</annotation></semantics></math>, applies an element-wise non-linear activation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}(\cdot):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}(\cdot):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and projects it back, as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
