<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{FF}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>FF</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{FF}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{FF}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>FF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{FF}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable weight matrices, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{FF}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>FF</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{FF}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable biases.
<math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>FF</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically set as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4d_{\mathrm{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4d_{\mathrm{model}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One interpretation of the FF layer is a knowledge retrieval module; that is, the module first creates <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS3.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.6" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.84px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 115.46 16.6" width="115.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(57.73,0) translate(0,4.84)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9D9FF;" fill="#D9D9FF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 53.58 11.76 L -53.58 11.76 C -55.87 11.76 -57.73 9.9 -57.73 7.61 L -57.73 -0.69 C -57.73 -2.98 -55.87 -4.84 -53.58 -4.84 L 53.58 -4.84 C 55.87 -4.84 57.73 -2.98 57.73 -0.69 L 57.73 7.61 C 57.73 9.9 55.87 11.76 53.58 11.76 Z M -57.73 -4.84"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -53.12 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="106.24"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">keys (activations)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> from an input <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>FF</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and then aggregates their associated <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS3.p1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="16.6" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.84px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 150.13 16.6" width="150.13"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(75.07,0) translate(0,4.84)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E2E2E2;" fill="#E2E2E2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 70.91 11.76 L -70.91 11.76 C -73.21 11.76 -75.07 9.9 -75.07 7.61 L -75.07 -0.69 C -75.07 -2.98 -73.21 -4.84 -70.91 -4.84 L 70.91 -4.84 C 73.21 -4.84 75.07 -2.98 75.07 -0.69 L 75.07 7.61 C 75.07 9.9 73.21 11.76 70.91 11.76 Z M -75.07 -4.84"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -70.45 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.18em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="140.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.pic2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">values (feature vectors)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>.
Existing studies have analyzed what kind of concept is stored in each feature vector of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{W}_{V[:,i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{V[:,i]}</annotation></semantics></math> and when they are activated by <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}\bm{W}_{K})_{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>FF</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{in}}}\bm{W}_{K})_{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Comparing FF-KVs with SAEs</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The feed-forward key-value memory module (FF-KV) inherently performs the same operation as SAEs (although it is somewhat obvious, given that both adopt the MLP architectures): it first decomposes the neuron activations into feature vectors and then aggregates them.
This naturally raises a question about how similar the decomposition <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">naturally</span> made by FF-KVs is to that <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">learned</span> by the proxy module, e.g., SAEs.
We examine several variants of FF-KV-based feature discovery methods<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          See Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Implementation Details on FF-KVs ‣ Transformer Key-Value Memories Are Nearly as Interpretable as Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for the details on the implementations</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Methods</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">FF-KV.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The vanilla FF key-value memories are evaluated with the SAE evaluation framework, treating the <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="15.07" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.07px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 99.24 15.07" width="99.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.07) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(49.62,0) translate(0,4.07)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9D9FF;" fill="#D9D9FF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 45.47 10.99 L -45.47 10.99 C -47.76 10.99 -49.62 9.13 -49.62 6.84 L -49.62 0.08 C -49.62 -2.22 -47.76 -4.07 -45.47 -4.07 L 45.47 -4.07 C 47.76 -4.07 49.62 -2.22 49.62 0.08 L 49.62 6.84 C 49.62 9.13 47.76 10.99 45.47 10.99 Z M -49.62 -4.07"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -45.01 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.51em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="90.02"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">key activations</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> as features and the <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="12.38" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.38px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 87.36 12.38" width="87.36"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,12.38) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(43.68,0) translate(0,1.38)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E2E2E2;" fill="#E2E2E2"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 39.53 10.99 L -39.53 10.99 C -41.82 10.99 -43.68 9.13 -43.68 6.84 L -43.68 2.77 C -43.68 0.47 -41.82 -1.38 -39.53 -1.38 L 39.53 -1.38 C 41.82 -1.38 43.68 0.47 43.68 2.77 L 43.68 6.84 C 43.68 9.13 41.82 10.99 39.53 10.99 Z M -43.68 -1.38"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -39.07 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="78.14"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.pic2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">value vectors</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> as feature vectors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TopK FF-KV.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To encourage the alignment with SAE research, we also introduce sparsity to activations in FFs by applying a top-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> activation function to the key vector, although it has been reported that the vanilla FFs’ activations are somewhat already sparse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
This keeps only the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> neurons with the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> largest activations in each inference, zeroing out the activation for the rest.
We call this <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TopK FF-KV</span>, defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>FF</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{x}_{\mathrm{FF_{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
