<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Detailed Results on FF Scaling</h2>

<figure id="A5.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.22332v1/cos_sim_annotation_threshold.svg" id="A5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/152;" width="238" height="152"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Proportion of aligned features as a function of the max-cosine score (MCS).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix E Detailed Results on FF Scaling ‣ Transformer Key-Value Memories Are Nearly as Interpretable as Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the results on all metrics regarding to various FF intermediate sizes. Scores are not showing noticeable improvement except for RAVEL and absorption. Trends shown in SCR somehow understandable: these metrics highly depend on the ground truth probing performance, which is not always stable. Sparse probing result is also understandable, since sparse probing on FF from a random transfer can achieve a reasonable score, the probs can learn unintended signal in the dataset, rather than the true feature.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T6" class="ltx_table">
